1: --- Guide for component-specific-light-dark-theme ---
2: # Component-specific light/dark themes
3: 
4: While more commonly set on the root, the `color-scheme` property can be set on individual elements to force them into a different color scheme from the rest of the page.
5: This can be useful for components that must always be viewed in a specific color scheme (e.g. always in dark or light mode).
6: 
7: Example use cases include:
8: - Elements that are often in dark mode even on light mode pages for aesthetic reasons, e.g. code blocks, media players, photo galleries
9: - Areas that contain media designed for a light background (e.g. images, videos, illustrations, print previews) can be set to light mode even if the rest of the page is in dark mode.
10: - Elements whose color-scheme is controlled by a user-level setting, such as component previews
11: - Embeds that don't support both light and dark modes
12: - Design tools, maps, visualizations, games etc.
13: 
14: ## When to change colors vs. when to force `color-scheme`?
15: 
16: Not every element that uses lighter text on darker background in light mode or darker text on lighter background in dark mode needs a different `color-scheme`.
17: For example, a primary button may be rendered as blue with white text in light mode, but that does not warrant a `color-scheme: dark`.
18: 
19: As a rule of thumb, typically elements using a different `color-scheme` are complex surfaces establishing their own visual context, rather than simple shallow containers.
20: 
21: When considering using a different `color-scheme` on an element, ask yourself:
22: 
23: - Should built-in browser UI that is not otherwise customized (e.g. form controls, scrollbars, etc) use that color-scheme or adapt to the page's color-scheme? -> if the former, don't use `color-scheme`.
24: - Should any `light-dark()` colors resolve like they do for the rest of the page or based on the override? ->
